+/**
+ * Put a clip's feet back where the animator put them.
+ *
+ * Retargeting a clip is done by copying rotation tracks across, and rotations
+ * carry pose rather than position. Two rigs holding identical joint angles put
+ * their feet in different places, because the places also depend on where the
+ * joints sit at rest - and these rigs disagree there by about six degrees at
+ * the hip and the ankle both. Six degrees at the hip is nine centimetres at
+ * the foot, which is the difference between a foot standing on the floor and a
+ * foot hovering beside where it should have stood.
+ *
+ * What that costs is measurable and large. On the source rig, walk-forward has
+ * each foot on the floor for 61% of the cycle with a quarter of the cycle on
+ * both feet at once, which is a walk. Copied onto an avatar, the same clip has
+ * a foot down 45% and 26% of the time, never both at once, and 29% of the
+ * cycle with neither foot down - which is a run, and a bad one. Everything
+ * downstream then works on a gait that was already broken: the plant hauls
+ * each foot an average of eight centimetres sideways to make a stance exist,
+ * and the ankle is wrenched to whatever angle that leaves.
+ *
+ * So the feet are solved rather than copied. The source clip's foot goals come
+ * in as `footprint`, in leg lengths from the hips and off the floor, and each
+ * sample is solved onto this rig's legs with the same IK the runtime plant
+ * uses. Contacts get the extra constraint that makes them contacts: the ball
+ * of the foot is held high enough that the front of the shoe rests on the
+ * floor rather than through it.
